"""Explicit finite models for probability source protocols. No empirical fallback."""
import math
from math_ops import op,OPS

op('monty','Doors','Switch Stay','[1-1/p[0],1/p[0]]',lambda p:[1-1/p[0],1/p[0]],'A toy prize is uniform over {Doors} doors. A knowledgeable host always reveals all but one unchosen goat door. What is the win probability if you switch?','The host preserves the probability of the entire initially unchosen set. This requires the stated knowledgeable-host protocol.','grid')
op('birthday','People Days','Collision','[1-Array.from({length:p[0]},(_,i)=>Math.max(0,1-i/p[1])).reduce((a,b)=>a*b,1)]',lambda p:[1-math.prod(max(0,1-i/p[1]) for i in range(int(p[0])))],'With {People} independent uniform birthdays across {Days} days, what is the probability of any shared birthday?','Multiply the successive probabilities of no match, then take the complement. The uniform independent calendar is an explicit assumption.','grid')
op('anyevent','P Trials','AtLeastOne','[1-Math.pow(1-p[0],p[1])]',lambda p:[1-(1-p[0])**p[1]],'An event independently occurs with probability {P} on each of {Trials} trials. What is the probability of at least one occurrence?','The complement is no occurrence on every trial. Independence is required for the product used here.','grid')
op('reportchild','Days','BothBoys','[(2*p[0]-1)/(4*p[0]-1)]',lambda p:[(2*p[0]-1)/(4*p[0]-1)],'Two children have independent sex and uniform birthdays across {Days} equally likely day categories. Condition on at least one boy in a specified category. What is the probability of two boys?','This conditions on the stated family-level event, rather than hearing about a randomly selected child. Reporting protocols change the sample space.','grid')
op('envelopes','Small PSmall','ExpectedGain','[p[0]*(2*p[1]-1)]',lambda p:[p[0]*(2*p[1]-1)],'Two toy envelopes contain {Small} and twice that amount. Without observing the contents, your probability of holding the smaller is {PSmall}. What is expected cash gain from switching?','The gain when initially holding the smaller amount balances the loss when holding the larger under uniform assignment. The assignment probability must be stated.')
op('awake','Prior HeadsVisits TailsVisits','HeadsGivenVisit','[p[0]*p[1]/(p[0]*p[1]+(1-p[0])*p[2])]',lambda p:[p[0]*p[1]/(p[0]*p[1]+(1-p[0])*p[2])],'A toy experiment has heads probability {Prior}, {HeadsVisits} visits after heads and {TailsVisits} after tails. Sampling a visit uniformly from repeated experiments, what fraction come from heads?','Sampling visits weights experiments by their number of visits. This resolves the stated sampling question, not every philosophical formulation.','grid')
op('chord','Threshold RadiusMode','Longer','[p[1]===0?p[0]:p[0]*p[0]]',lambda p:[p[0] if p[1]==0 else p[0]**2],'A toy chord has midpoint radius r in a unit disk. It is long when r is below {Threshold}. Mode {RadiusMode} uses radius-uniform if 0, area-uniform if 1. What is the long-chord probability?','A random procedure needs a measure. Radius-uniform and area-uniform midpoint sampling assign different probabilities to the same geometric event.','grid')
op('runwait','P Pattern','Expected','[p[1]===0?(1+p[0])/(p[0]*p[0]):1/(p[0]*(1-p[0]))]',lambda p:[(1+p[0])/p[0]**2 if p[1]==0 else 1/(p[0]*(1-p[0]))],'Independent flips have heads probability {P}. Pattern {Pattern} means HH if 0, HT if 1. What is the expected waiting time from no prefix?','Overlapping prefixes alter the waiting time. Both patterns have the same one-window probability for a fair coin but different restart behavior.','grid')
op('secretary','N Skip','Success','[p[1]/p[0]*Array.from({length:p[0]-p[1]},(_,i)=>1/(p[1]+i)).reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0)]',lambda p:[p[1]/p[0]*sum(1/i for i in range(int(p[1]),int(p[0])))],'There are {N} randomly ordered distinct candidates. Skip {Skip}, then choose the next record, or the last if none. What is the probability of selecting the best?','The stopping threshold trades off learning the scale and leaving enough candidates. The formula assumes random order and a single irrevocable selection.','grid')
op('sizebias','ValueA CountA ValueB CountB','Weighted Ordinary','[(p[0]*p[0]*p[1]+p[2]*p[2]*p[3])/(p[0]*p[1]+p[2]*p[3]),(p[0]*p[1]+p[2]*p[3])/(p[1]+p[3])]',lambda p:[(p[0]**2*p[1]+p[2]**2*p[3])/(p[0]*p[1]+p[2]*p[3]),(p[0]*p[1]+p[2]*p[3])/(p[1]+p[3])],'A toy population has {CountA} units of size {ValueA} and {CountB} of size {ValueB}. Sampling units proportional to size, what mean size is seen?','Size-weighted sampling overrepresents large units. Compare it with the ordinary mean over units before interpreting an apparent paradox.','grid')
op('coupon','Types','Expected','[p[0]*Array.from({length:p[0]},(_,i)=>1/(i+1)).reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0)]',lambda p:[p[0]*sum(1/i for i in range(1,int(p[0])+1))],'Each independent toy pack contains one uniformly chosen type among {Types}. What is the expected number of packs to collect all types?','The last missing types take longer to find than the first. The expectation sums waiting times at each collection stage.','grid')
op('runs','N P','ExpectedRuns','[1+2*(p[0]-1)*p[1]*(1-p[1])]',lambda p:[1+2*(p[0]-1)*p[1]*(1-p[1])],'A sequence has {N} independent flips with heads probability {P}. A run is a maximal block of identical results. What is the expected number of runs?','Count the first block plus each transition. A run summary can challenge a proposed random generator without proving how a person generated a particular sequence.','grid')
op('dice','Sides Sum','Probability','[Math.max(0,Math.min(p[1]-1,2*p[0]+1-p[1],p[0]))/(p[0]*p[0])]',lambda p:[max(0,min(p[1]-1,2*p[0]+1-p[1],p[0]))/p[0]**2],'Roll two independent uniform {Sides}-sided dice numbered from 1. What is the probability their sum is {Sum}?','Count ordered pairs, not just possible sums. Middle sums can be formed by more pairs.','grid')
op('benford','Digit','Probability','[Math.log10(1+1/p[0])]',lambda p:[math.log10(1+1/p[0])],'A toy base-ten leading digit follows the Benford model. What probability is assigned to digit {Digit}?','A uniform logarithmic mantissa yields unequal leading-digit shares. The model does not apply to every numerical dataset.','grid')
op('logfactorial','Cards','Log10Orders','[Array.from({length:p[0]},(_,i)=>Math.log10(i+1)).reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0)]',lambda p:[sum(math.log10(i) for i in range(1,int(p[0])+1))],'A toy deck has {Cards} distinct cards. What is log10 of the number of possible orders?','The factorial counts permutations of distinct cards. A logarithmic output keeps a huge exact count within a readable numeric range.','grid')
op('lcg','Seed A C Modulus','Next','[(p[1]*p[0]+p[2])%p[3]]',lambda p:[(p[1]*p[0]+p[2])%p[3]],'A toy generator updates x as (A {A} × x + C {C}) modulo {Modulus}, starting at Seed {Seed}. What is the next state?','A seed and deterministic recurrence reproduce a sequence. Reproducibility is distinct from unpredictability or statistical quality.','grid')
op('posteriorodds','Prior LR','Posterior','[p[0]*p[1]/(1-p[0]+p[0]*p[1])]',lambda p:[p[0]*p[1]/(1-p[0]+p[0]*p[1])],'A toy hypothesis has prior probability {Prior}; evidence has likelihood ratio {LR} in its favor. What is its posterior probability?','A likelihood ratio multiplies prior odds, rather than replacing the prior probability. The likelihood model must be specified.','grid')
op('testrepeat','Prior Sensitivity FalsePositive Tests','Posterior','[p[0]*Math.pow(p[1],p[3])/(p[0]*Math.pow(p[1],p[3])+(1-p[0])*Math.pow(p[2],p[3]))]',lambda p:[p[0]*p[1]**p[3]/(p[0]*p[1]**p[3]+(1-p[0])*p[2]**p[3])],'Toy prevalence is {Prior}; sensitivity {Sensitivity}, false-positive rate {FalsePositive}. Given {Tests} positive tests independent conditional on status, what is posterior probability?','Conditional independence permits multiplication within each status. Repeating a correlated test need not provide the same evidence.','grid')
op('negative','Prior Sensitivity FalsePositive','Posterior','[p[0]*(1-p[1])/(p[0]*(1-p[1])+(1-p[0])*(1-p[2]))]',lambda p:[p[0]*(1-p[1])/(p[0]*(1-p[1])+(1-p[0])*(1-p[2]))],'Toy prevalence is {Prior}, sensitivity {Sensitivity} and false-positive rate {FalsePositive}. After a negative result, what is the probability of the condition?','A negative result reweights cases and noncases by their probabilities of testing negative. A test result alone does not fix its predictive meaning.','grid')
op('poisson','Rate Count','Probability','[Math.exp(-p[0])*Math.pow(p[0],p[1])/Array.from({length:p[1]},(_,i)=>i+1).reduce((a,b)=>a*b,1)]',lambda p:[math.exp(-p[0])*p[0]**p[1]/math.factorial(int(p[1]))],'A toy Poisson count has mean {Rate}. What is the probability of exactly {Count} events?','The mean fixes this Poisson distribution. Agreement at one count does not establish that real events satisfy the model.','grid')
op('exponential','Rate Time','Survival Mean','[Math.exp(-p[0]*p[1]),1/p[0]]',lambda p:[math.exp(-p[0]*p[1]),1/p[0]],'A toy exponential waiting time has rate {Rate}. What is the probability the wait exceeds {Time}?','A constant hazard gives exponential survival and memorylessness. A real waiting process need not have a constant hazard.','grid')
op('betamean','Alpha Beta Wins Losses','Mean','[(p[0]+p[2])/(p[0]+p[1]+p[2]+p[3])]',lambda p:[(p[0]+p[2])/(p[0]+p[1]+p[2]+p[3])],'A toy coin has Beta({Alpha},{Beta}) prior and independent data of {Wins} wins and {Losses} losses. What is the posterior predictive win probability?','Prior counts and observed counts add under this model. The posterior mean also gives the next-trial predictive probability.','grid')
op('pareto','TopShare Alpha','WealthShare','[Math.pow(p[0],1-1/p[1])]',lambda p:[p[0]**(1-1/p[1])],'A toy Pareto distribution has shape Alpha {Alpha} greater than 1. What fraction of total wealth is held by the top population share {TopShare}?','The shape parameter controls concentration and requires a finite mean for this formula. It is a stated distribution, not an estimate of a country.','grid')
op('normalcoverage','Z','Coverage','[1-Math.exp(-0.717*p[0]-0.416*p[0]*p[0])]',lambda p:[1-math.exp(-.717*p[0]-.416*p[0]**2)],'Use the explicit toy approximation C(z)=1-exp(-0.717z-0.416z²). At nonnegative z {Z}, what central normal coverage does it approximate?','This visible approximation illustrates central coverage; it is not an exact normal CDF or a confidence statement about a particular unknown parameter.','grid')
op('pollse','P N Population','SE','[Math.sqrt(p[0]*(1-p[0])/p[1]*(p[2]-p[1])/(p[2]-1))]',lambda p:[math.sqrt(p[0]*(1-p[0])/p[1]*(p[2]-p[1])/(p[2]-1))],'A simple random toy poll samples {N} without replacement from population {Population} with proportion {P}. What is the standard error of its proportion?','The finite-population correction depends on the sampling fraction. Large populations can have nearly equal sampling errors at the same sample size.','grid')
op('normalshrink','Prior Variance Measurement Noise','Mean Variance','[(p[2]/p[3]+p[0]/p[1])/(1/p[3]+1/p[1]),1/(1/p[3]+1/p[1])]',lambda p:[(p[2]/p[3]+p[0]/p[1])/(1/p[3]+1/p[1]),1/(1/p[3]+1/p[1])],'A toy normal prior has mean {Prior}, variance {Variance}; one normal measurement is {Measurement} with noise variance {Noise}. What is the posterior mean?','Precisions weight the prior and measurement. The variance calculation assumes the stated independent normal model.','grid')
op('regression','Mean Observed Reliability','ExpectedNext','[p[0]+p[2]*(p[1]-p[0])]',lambda p:[p[0]+p[2]*(p[1]-p[0])],'A toy repeat-score model predicts Mean {Mean} plus Reliability {Reliability} times the deviation of Observed {Observed}. What is the expected next score?','Selecting an extreme noisy score can be followed by a less extreme score without any intervention effect. This prediction rule is explicitly assumed.')
op('samplesize','P Z Error','N','[Math.ceil(p[0]*(1-p[0])*p[1]*p[1]/(p[2]*p[2]))]',lambda p:[math.ceil(p[0]*(1-p[0])*p[1]**2/p[2]**2)],'Use the toy large-population normal approximation with proportion {P}, multiplier {Z}, and desired half-width {Error}. What rounded-up sample size is required?','Precision improves with the square root of sample size. The calculation assumes a simple random sample and ignores nonresponse and design effects.','grid')
op('tank','Maximum Sample','Estimate','[p[0]*(p[1]+1)/p[1]-1]',lambda p:[p[0]*(p[1]+1)/p[1]-1],'A simple random sample without replacement of {Sample} serials from 1 through N has maximum {Maximum}. What is the unbiased tank-count estimate?','The gap above the observed maximum must be estimated. Uniform sampling of distinct serials is central to this estimator.','grid')
op('likelihoodpower','Prior P1 P0 Heads','Posterior','[p[0]*Math.pow(p[1],p[3])/(p[0]*Math.pow(p[1],p[3])+(1-p[0])*Math.pow(p[2],p[3]))]',lambda p:[p[0]*p[1]**p[3]/(p[0]*p[1]**p[3]+(1-p[0])*p[2]**p[3])],'Toy hypothesis H1 has prior {Prior} and heads chance {P1}; H0 has heads chance {P0}. After {Heads} independent heads, what is P(H1)?','Evidence compares how both hypotheses predict the same data. A high likelihood under one hypothesis is insufficient without the alternative and prior.','grid')
op('kelly','P Odds','Fraction','[p[0]-(1-p[0])/p[1]]',lambda p:[p[0]-(1-p[0])/p[1]],'A toy repeatable bet wins with probability {P}, paying net Odds {Odds} per unit staked. What unconstrained Kelly fraction maximizes expected log growth?','The optimum depends on a known probability and payoff model. Estimation error, constraints and risk preferences can justify a different stake.','grid')
op('tworeturns','Up Down','Factor','[(1+p[0])*(1+p[1])]',lambda p:[(1+p[0])*(1+p[1])],'A toy investment returns {Up} in one period and {Down} in the next. What final-to-initial wealth factor results?','Returns multiply through the changing balance. Their arithmetic average need not describe realized compound growth.','grid')
op('ruin','Bankroll Total P','Ruin','[p[2]===0.5?1-p[0]/p[1]:1-(1-Math.pow((1-p[2])/p[2],p[0]))/(1-Math.pow((1-p[2])/p[2],p[1]))]',lambda p:[1-p[0]/p[1] if p[2]==.5 else 1-(1-((1-p[2])/p[2])**p[0])/(1-((1-p[2])/p[2])**p[1])],'A toy unit-step gambler starts at {Bankroll}, stops at 0 or {Total}, and wins each independent step with probability {P}. What is the probability of reaching 0 first?','A favorable one-step chance does not eliminate ruin at an absorbing boundary. Bankroll and target both matter.','grid')
op('doubling','Stake Losses','Required Next','[p[0]*(Math.pow(2,p[1])-1),p[0]*Math.pow(2,p[1])]',lambda p:[p[0]*(2**p[1]-1),p[0]*2**p[1]],'A toy doubling strategy begins at stake {Stake}. After {Losses} consecutive losses, how much total has been lost?','Losses accumulate geometrically. A finite bankroll or table limit prevents indefinite doubling.','grid')
op('overround','OddsA OddsB','Excess','[1/p[0]+1/p[1]-1]',lambda p:[1/p[0]+1/p[1]-1],'Two exhaustive outcomes have toy decimal odds {OddsA} and {OddsB}. What is the sum of implied probabilities minus one?','The reciprocal-odds total describes the quoted margin. It is not automatically the realized house profit on every set of stakes.','grid')
op('chain','ToRain ToSun StartRain Steps','Rain','[p[0]/(p[0]+p[1])+(p[2]-p[0]/(p[0]+p[1]))*Math.pow(1-p[0]-p[1],p[3])]',lambda p:[p[0]/(p[0]+p[1])+(p[2]-p[0]/(p[0]+p[1]))*(1-p[0]-p[1])**p[3]],'A toy chain switches sun to rain with chance {ToRain}, rain to sun with {ToSun}; initial rain probability {StartRain}. What is rain probability after {Steps} steps?','Transition probabilities determine both the stationary share and how the initial condition fades. This formula is for the stated two-state chain.','grid')
op('queue','Arrival Service','MeanTime','[1/(p[1]-p[0])]',lambda p:[1/(p[1]-p[0])],'A stable toy M/M/1 queue has Poisson arrival rate {Arrival} and exponential service rate {Service}. What is mean time in the system?','The gap between service and arrival rates controls this queue’s mean delay. Variability and the assumed queueing model matter.','grid')
op('ballot','A B','Lead','[(p[0]-p[1])/(p[0]+p[1])]',lambda p:[(p[0]-p[1])/(p[0]+p[1])],'A has {A} votes and B has {B}, with A greater. In a uniformly random count order, what is the chance A stays strictly ahead after every count?','The strict ballot event excludes ties at any intermediate count. Final victory alone does not imply a lead throughout.','grid')
op('branch','Zero Two','Extinction','[Math.min(1,p[0]/p[1])]',lambda p:[min(1,p[0]/p[1])],'Each toy lineage independently has zero descendants with probability {Zero}, two with {Two}, otherwise one. What is its eventual extinction probability?','The smallest fixed point of the offspring generating function gives extinction. Growing mean population does not guarantee survival of every lineage.','grid')
op('urn','Red Blue Draws','ExpectedRedShare','[p[0]/(p[0]+p[1])]',lambda p:[p[0]/(p[0]+p[1])],'A toy Polya urn starts with {Red} red and {Blue} blue. Each of {Draws} draws returns the ball and adds one of its color. What is the expected red share?','The expected share remains the initial share, even though individual reinforced paths can diverge. Expectation and concentration are separate properties.','grid')
op('mcse','Hits N Scale','Estimate SE','[p[2]*p[0]/p[1],p[2]*Math.sqrt((p[0]/p[1])*(1-p[0]/p[1])/p[1])]',lambda p:[p[2]*p[0]/p[1],p[2]*math.sqrt((p[0]/p[1])*(1-p[0]/p[1])/p[1])],'A toy Monte Carlo sample has {Hits} hits among {N}; multiply the hit fraction by Scale {Scale}. What estimate results?','Sampling error remains after a Monte Carlo estimate. The reported plug-in standard error assumes independent indicator draws.','grid')
op('inverseexp','U Rate','Time','[-Math.log(1-p[0])/p[1]]',lambda p:[-math.log(1-p[0])/p[1]],'Transform a toy uniform value U {U} by -log(1-U)/Rate {Rate}. What exponential waiting time results?','Applying an inverse CDF maps uniform draws into the specified distribution. Values must lie inside the stated uniform domain.','grid')
op('importance','EventTarget EventProposal','Weight','[p[0]/p[1]]',lambda p:[p[0]/p[1]],'A toy event has target probability {EventTarget} but proposal probability {EventProposal}. What likelihood weight corrects a sampled event indicator?','Oversampling an event needs a compensating likelihood ratio. Unweighted proposal frequencies would estimate the wrong distribution.','grid')
op('anneal','Increase Temperature','Acceptance','[Math.exp(-p[0]/p[1])]',lambda p:[math.exp(-p[0]/p[1])],'A toy annealing move increases cost by {Increase}, with positive temperature {Temperature}. What acceptance probability does exp(-increase/temperature) assign?','A higher temperature permits more uphill moves. This one-step rule alone does not guarantee a global optimum.','grid')
op('logistic','Score','Probability','[1/(1+Math.exp(-p[0]))]',lambda p:[1/(1+math.exp(-p[0]))],'A toy logistic model has score {Score}. What probability is 1/(1+exp(-score))?','The link maps an unbounded score into a bounded model probability. Calibration still requires outcome evidence.','curve')
op('temperature','LogitA LogitB Temperature','ProbabilityA','[1/(1+Math.exp((p[1]-p[0])/p[2]))]',lambda p:[1/(1+math.exp((p[1]-p[0])/p[2]))],'Two toy logits are {LogitA} and {LogitB}, divided by positive Temperature {Temperature} before softmax. What probability is assigned to A?','Temperature changes sampling concentration while preserving logit order. It does not supply evidence that the scores are calibrated.','grid')
op('confusion','TP FP FN TN','Precision Recall Accuracy','[p[0]/(p[0]+p[1]),p[0]/(p[0]+p[2]),(p[0]+p[3])/(p[0]+p[1]+p[2]+p[3])]',lambda p:[p[0]/(p[0]+p[1]),p[0]/(p[0]+p[2]),(p[0]+p[3])/sum(p)],'A toy classifier records TP {TP}, FP {FP}, FN {FN}, TN {TN}. What is its precision?','Precision conditions on positive predictions; recall conditions on actual positives. Accuracy can hide poor performance on a rare class.','grid')
op('errorcost','FP FN CostFP CostFN','Cost','[p[0]*p[2]+p[1]*p[3]]',lambda p:[p[0]*p[2]+p[1]*p[3]],'A toy decision rule makes {FP} false positives and {FN} false negatives, costing {CostFP} and {CostFN} each. What is its total error cost?','Changing error costs can change the preferred rule even on identical outcomes. The costs are explicit decision assumptions.','grid')

